 — Calculate MSE\n7.7 — Compare with sklearn\n7.8 — Understand what we learned\n\nThis is where you'll start understanding what Linear Regression is actually doing internally, rather than treating LinearRegression() as a black box.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"STEP 7 — LINEAR REGRESSION FROM SCRATCH\n│\n├── 7.1 — Understand the Linear Regression Equation\n├── 7.2 — Prepare the Feature Matrix\n├── 7.3 — Add the Intercept\n├── 7.4 — Calculate Coefficients Using the Normal Equation\n├── 7.5 — Make Predictions\n├── 7.6 — Calculate MSE\n├── 7.7 — Compare With sklearn\n└── 7.8 — Verify the Results","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"7.1 — Understand the Linear Regression Equation\nThe basic equation is:\n\\[\n\\hat{y} = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2x_2 + \\cdots + \\beta_nx_n\n\\]\nIn matrix form:\n\\[\n\\hat{y} = X\\beta\n\\]\nWe'll use the Normal Equation to calculate the coefficients:\n\\[\n\\beta = (X^TX)^{-1}X^Ty\n\\]\nFor numerical stability, we'll use the pseudoinverse:\n\\[\n\\beta = X^+y\n\\]\nwhere \\(X^+\\) is the Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ============================================================\n# STEP 7 — LINEAR REGRESSION FROM SCRATCH\n# 7.2 — Prepare the Feature Matrix\n# ============================================================\n\n# OBJECTIVE: Convert our preprocessed training data into a NumPy\n# matrix so we can perform the mathematical operations required\n# to calculate Linear Regression coefficients ourselves.\n\n\nimport numpy as np\n\n\n# Convert the processed training DataFrame into a NumPy array.\n#\n# .values\n#     → extracts the numerical values from the DataFrame.\n#\n# NumPy arrays allow us to perform matrix multiplication and\n# other mathematical operations efficiently.\nX_train_scratch = X_train_processed_df.values\n\n\n# Convert the training target into a NumPy array.\n#\n# y_train contains the actual house prices that the model\n# needs to learn from.\ny_train_scratch = y_train.values\n\n\n# Display the shapes of the matrices.\nprint(\"===== FROM-SCRATCH DATA =====\")\nprint(\"X_train shape:\", X_train_scratch.shape)\nprint(\"y_train shape:\", y_train_scratch.shape)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-02T12:50:51.858767Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-02T12:50:51.859269Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-02T12:50:51.86485Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-02T12:50:51.859236Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-02T12:50:51.864141Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"===== FROM-SCRATCH DATA =====\nX_train shape: (436, 20)\ny_train shape: (436,)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":47},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ============================================================\n# STEP 7 — LINEAR REGRESSION FROM SCRATCH\n# 7.3 — Add the Intercept\n# ============================================================\n\n# OBJECTIVE: Add a column of ones to the feature matrix so that\n# the intercept (β₀) can be learned together with all other\n# Linear Regression coefficients.\n\n\n# Create a column containing only 1s.\n#\n# The number of rows must match the number of training houses.\n#\n# X_train_scratch.shape[0]\n#     → gives the number of rows in X_train_scratch.\nintercept_column = np.ones(\n    (X_train_scratch.shape[0], 1)\n)\n\n\n# Add the column of ones to the beginning of our feature matrix.\n#\n# np.hstack()\n#     → horizontally joins arrays.\n#\n# The resulting matrix looks like:\n#\n# [1, x1, x2, x3, ...]\n#\n# The first column represents the intercept.\nX_train_with_intercept = np.hstack(\n    [intercept_column, X_train_scratch]\n)\n\n\n# Display the new shape.\nprint(\"===== FEATURE MATRIX WITH INTERCEPT =====\")\nprint(\n    \"X_train_with_intercept shape:\",\n    X_train_with_intercept.shape\n)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-02T12:51:00.698204Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-02T12:51:00.69854Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-02T12:51:00.70408Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-02T12:51:00.698513Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-02T12:51:00.703465Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"===== FEATURE MATRIX WITH INTERCEPT =====\nX_train_with_intercept shape: (436, 21)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":48},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ============================================================\n# STEP 7 — LINEAR REGRESSION FROM SCRATCH\n# 7.4 — Calculate Coefficients Using the Normal Equation\n# ============================================================\n\n# OBJECTIVE: Calculate the Linear Regression coefficients directly\n# from the training data using the mathematical Normal Equation.\n\n\n# Calculate the pseudoinverse of the feature matrix.\n#\n# np.linalg.pinv()\n#     → calculates the Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse.\n#\n# We use the pseudoinverse instead of directly calculating\n# (XᵀX)⁻¹ because it is generally more numerically stable,\n# especially when features are correlated.\nX_pseudo_inverse = np.linalg.pinv(\n    X_train_with_intercept\n)\n\n\n# Calculate all Linear Regression coefficients.\n#\n# Mathematical form:\n#\n# β = X⁺y\n#\n# X⁺ → pseudoinverse of X\n# y  → actual training prices\n#\n# The first coefficient will be the intercept.\n# The remaining coefficients correspond to our features.\nbeta = X_pseudo_inverse @ y_train_scratch\n\n\n# Display the number of learned coefficients.\nprint(\"===== LEARNED COEFFICIENTS =====\")\nprint(\"Number of coefficients:\", len(beta))\n\n\n# Display the first few coefficients.\nprint(\"\\nFirst 10 coefficients:\")\nprint(beta[:10])","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-02T12:51:13.318956Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-02T12:51:13.319762Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-02T12:51:13.338753Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-02T12:51:13.319721Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-02T12:51:13.33772Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdo